% ============================================================================
% compound-speleothem-surface — which cave surfaces a compound speleothem
% reaches, DERIVED through its components (adj-facts-stdlib, EARTH-SCIENCE).
% ============================================================================
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? compound_speleothem_surface(column, $S)
%       % cave_ceiling
%       % cave_floor
%
% No table states this. It is DERIVED, by a `rule` whose body chains the
% component relation with the growth-surface relation:
%
%     speleothem_component(column, stalactite)
%     speleothem_growth_surface(stalactite, cave_ceiling)
%     ---------------------------------------------------
%     compound_speleothem_surface(column, cave_ceiling)
%
%     speleothem_component(column, stalagmite)
%     speleothem_growth_surface(stalagmite, cave_floor)
%     ---------------------------------------------------
%     compound_speleothem_surface(column, cave_floor)
%
% Two premises, two answers, each carrying the citations of the facts that
% produced it.
%
% *** THIS IS THE OTHER HALF OF AN ABSTENTION, AND THE POINT OF THE PAIR. ***
%
% `speleothem-growth-surface.adj` refused to give `column` a value. Its
% header states the reason precisely: recording `cave_ceiling` or
% `cave_floor` "would be false, and recording both would misrepresent the
% relation this table holds". That was right. `speleothem_growth_surface`
% means THE SURFACE A SPELEOTHEM GROWS FROM, and a column does not grow
% from a surface at all — it is two speleothems that met.
%
% But the reader's question — "does a column touch the ceiling or the
% floor?" — is a good question with a grounded answer. It was never
% unanswerable; it was merely not a question about growth surfaces. This
% rule answers it in a relation that MEANS what the answer means: the
% surfaces reached by this speleothem's components. Two answers is correct
% here, where two rows would have been a misrepresentation there, because
% the relations are not the same relation.
%
% That distinction is the whole argument for abstaining rather than
% fudging. A table that had quietly bound both surfaces would have been
% approximately useful and precisely wrong, and nothing downstream could
% have recovered the difference. Because it declined, the honest answer
% remained available — as a composition, in its own name, with its
% provenance intact.
%
% The chain must be a RULE rather than a two-goal query: a query in this
% language is a single term. Same shape as
% `karst-process-water-table-position.adj` and `civics/chamber-branch.adj`.
%
% Provenance: the rule's envelope cites the sentence that establishes a
% column as constituted by the pair, on the U.S. National Park Service's
% "Speleothems" page:
%
%     "Columns are not stalactites nor are they stalagmites; they are
%      both, together."
%
% `trust authoritative`. Each derived answer additionally carries the
% citations of its two premises, so nothing it relied on goes uncited.
%
% *** A LIMITATION, STATED PLAINLY RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE DISCOVERED. ***
% Provenance in this stdlib is attached at TABLE level: a table's `source`
% envelope and its `cites` corroborations cover EVERY row, not the row that
% happened to match. So the two answers here are provenanced IDENTICALLY.
% The `cave_floor` answer carries the same citation list as the
% `cave_ceiling` one — with the stalactite sentence as its primary citation
% and the stalagmite sentence only as a corroboration — even though the
% stalactite row played no part in deriving it.
%
% This is worth saying because the tempting claim is the opposite one: that
% the ceiling answer traces to the stalactite sentence and the floor answer
% to the stalagmite one. An earlier draft of this library's test asserted
% exactly that, and the assertions PASSED — both sentences appear in the
% output of any successful use of the growth-surface table, so the test
% proved nothing while reading as though it proved per-answer attribution.
% A library about provenance honesty does not get to overclaim its own
% provenance. What holds is: BOTH PREMISES ARE CITED. What does not hold
% is: each answer is cited with the sentence that produced it.
%
% `facts_compoundspeleothemsurface_e2e.rs` pins the limitation with a
% floor-only query, which under row-level provenance would carry only the
% stalagmite sentence and today carries the stalactite one too. If that
% test ever fails, row-level provenance has landed and this header should
% be revisited with it.
%
% Honest abstention (inherited from the premises):
%
%   - Any speleothem `speleothem_component` does not decompose has no
%     components to chain through and abstains here. This relation is only
%     defined for compound speleothems, which on this page means `column`
%     alone.
%   - `helictite` abstains, and for a reason worth stating: it is absent
%     from BOTH premises. `speleothem-growth-surface.adj` declined it
%     because the source gives three surfaces with a frequency hedge
%     ("cave ceilings, walls, and less often on cave floors") that no
%     two-column row can carry. A derived relation cannot repair a premise
%     it inherits — composition propagates abstention exactly as it
%     propagates provenance.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

import "speleothem-component.adj"
import "speleothem-growth-surface.adj"

rule { head: compound_speleothem_surface($Compound, $Surface)
       when: speleothem_component($Compound, $Component), speleothem_growth_surface($Component, $Surface)
       source "Columns are not stalactites nor are they stalagmites; they are both, together."
       locator "https://www.nps.gov/subjects/caves/speleothems.htm"
       trust authoritative }
